xvfb-static: load a compiled-in keymap (no rules file, no xkbcomp)

Patches xkb/ddxLoad.c so XkbCompileKeymapForDevice() returns a keymap read
straight out of a `static const unsigned char xvfb_static_keymap_xkm[]` array
compiled into the binary, instead of building one from the filesystem.

Why the hook sits at XkbCompileKeymapForDevice and not further down
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Upstream's path is two filesystem-bound steps:

  XkbCompileKeymapForDevice
    -> XkbRMLVOtoKcCGST -> XkbDDXNamesFromRules   opens <xkbdir>/rules/evdev
    -> XkbDDXLoadKeymapByNames
         -> XkbDDXCompileKeymapByNames -> RunXkbComp   fork+execv xkbcomp
         -> LoadXKM -> XkmReadFile(FILE *)             reads, then unlinks

The rules lookup happens BEFORE anything is compiled, so a prebuilt .xkm alone
does not free the binary from the data tree: pointing -xkbdir at an empty
directory dies in XkbDDXNamesFromRules with "XKB: Failed to compile keymap",
which misleadingly blames the compile step. Hooking at
XkbCompileKeymapForDevice bypasses both steps at once.

This is tractable because LoadXKM reads through XkmReadFile(FILE *file, ...).
Since the reader takes a FILE *, fmemopen() feeds it the embedded bytes
directly -- no output directory, no temp file, no unlink, no process spawn, no
filesystem access. fmemopen() is POSIX.1-2008 and present in musl, this
project's only libc (pkgsStatic).

The fixed requirement mask, and why the caller's `need` must not be used
------------------------------------------------------------------------

On failure, upstream's KeymapOrDefaults() logs "Failed to load keymap. Loading
default keymap instead." and re-enters this function with need == 0. Re-reading
the same embedded blob only guarantees the same OUTCOME if the accept/reject
test is identical on both calls, and upstream's retained (need & provided) !=
need test evaluates to (0 & provided) != 0 on the retry -- always false,
leaving only the `xkb == NULL` guard.

That guard covers one of XkmReadFile()'s two rejection shapes. A bad TOC
returns before XkbAllocKeyboard(), but a bad SECTION past a valid TOC
ALLOCATES *xkb and returns it partially populated (xkmread.c:1242), so the
retry yields a non-NULL half-loaded XkbDescPtr and the server boots on it.
This patch makes that shape reachable, because upstream's retry recompiled the
default RMLVO from the data tree and normally succeeded.

So the verdict is tested against a FIXED local requirement
(`xvfb_static_need`), and that same fixed mask is passed to XkmReadFile(),
making this function's answer independent of what the caller asked for. Do not
reintroduce the caller's `need` here. See the function comment for the mask set.

Diagnostics use ErrorF(), not LogMessage()
------------------------------------------

LogMessage() logs at verbosity 1, logVerbosity defaults to 0 (os/log.c), and
Xvfb has no log file, so a LogMessage() diagnostic on this path is silently
dropped -- a fail-loud message nobody can see. ErrorF() routes to
LogVWrite(-1, ...) and always reaches stderr, which is what xkbInit.c already
does for this failure. The same gap is why upstream's "Loading default keymap
instead." warning is invisible here.

Second hook: reject the string-keymap entry point
-------------------------------------------------

XkbCompileKeymapFromString() returns NULL after saying so, rather than
delegating to KeymapOrDefaults(). Delegating would fall through to
XkbCompileKeymapForDevice(), succeed on the embedded keymap, and boot as though
-keymap had been honoured -- silently substituting a different layout than the
one explicitly requested. NULL instead routes into the caller's existing
failure handling (xkbInit.c logs "XKB: Failed to compile keymap" and unwinds,
ending in FatalError("Failed to activate virtual core keyboard")), so the
server refuses to start.

The path is latent rather than live. Xvfb's argument parser does not accept
-keymap at all; XkbCompileKeymapFromString()'s only caller is
InitKeyboardDeviceStructFromString() (xkb/xkbInit.c), an _X_EXPORT API
(include/input.h) with no in-tree caller in an Xvfb-only build -- upstream it
is Xwayland's path. The guard is kept because it is correct and free in the
unreachable case, and because closing the function is what allows the
now-unreferenced statics to be deleted with it.

Deletions
---------

Removed as unreferenced once their only caller was replaced, and left in place
would warn under -Wunused-function: XkbRMLVOtoKcCGST(),
xkb_write_keymap_string_cb(), and XkbDDXLoadKeymapFromString() with the
XkbKeymapString struct they shared. Their callees XkbDDXNamesFromRules() and
RunXkbComp() are exported entry points (include/xkbsrv.h) and stay.

RunXkbComp() now has no in-tree caller on any reachable path, which leaves
xserver-0001's XVFB_STATIC_XKB_BINDIR / XVFB_STATIC_XKM_OUTPUT_DIR overrides
and its shell-less direct-exec machinery dead weight. Retiring 0001 is a
separate decision, not a side effect of this one.

Scope
-----

The Xvfb binary serves only the curated embedded catalog selected by the VFB
parser. That is a deliberate capability reduction, not a side effect of a size
optimization. RMLVO arguments are ignored; the parameters remain because the
signature is fixed by both call sites in this file. bin/xkbcomp and share/xkb
become unreachable and are dropped from the artifact, along with a fork+execv
from every display start.

Generated against a tree with xserver-0001 already applied, which is the order
package.nix lists them in.

